## Run log

- 2026-10-15 13:24 UTC: branch created, draft config + launch yaml added, corpus and Qwen3-8B
  downloaded to the node. 3-step smoke on a 2,000-row slice (`training.max_steps=3`, no warmup):
  step 1 loss 3.08 (CE 12.94 / L1 2.00) acc 0.000, step 2 loss 7.07 acc 0.037, step 3 loss 3.01
  acc 0.014; optimizer step 6.5-6.7 s on six H200 ranks (22 micro-batches), trainer peak memory
  36 GB / rank, `*-step3` saved and exported to HF layout (model.safetensors 932 MB = 0.466B
  parameters, `intermediate_size` 1536, block 16).
- 2026-10-15 13:30 UTC: first launch with the dense-arm layout (2 capture servers + 6 ranks, global
  batch 132): 6.0 s per optimizer step (train compute 5.5 s, data wait 0.6 s), i.e. ~18 h per epoch;
  trainer GPUs at 100% while both capture servers sat in backpressure. Stopped at step 230 (13:51 UTC)
  to switch layouts; its W&B run was deleted (W&B then refuses the old id, so the relaunch uses run id
  `dense-1-8-1server-dp7`, display name `dense-1-8`).
- 2026-10-15 14:08 UTC: relaunched with 1 capture server + 7 trainer ranks (global batch 133,
  128,201 steps, checkpoints every 1,187 steps): 6.5-6.8 s per step but data wait 1.7-1.9 s and
  only 20 samples/s; the single capture process left GPU 0 idle ~60% of the time. Stopped at
  step ~80 (14:20 UTC).
- 2026-10-15 14:25 UTC: tried two SGLang capture processes on GPU 0 (7 ranks); the launch
  failed at startup (see below), and the decision was to go back to two capture GPUs.
- 2026-10-15 14:36 UTC: relaunched with the dense-arm layout (2 capture GPUs + 6 ranks, global
  batch 132, 129,172 steps, checkpoints every 1,196 steps; W&B run id `dense-1-8-r3`): 6.0-6.2 s
  per step, data wait 0.7-0.8 s.
- 2026-10-15 15:0x UTC: restarted from scratch with the DDP gradient-sync fix (`a08a24ec`, found
  by the MoE arm: DDP decides at forward time whether the next backward all-reduces, so
  `no_sync()` around backward alone never deferred the collective and every micro-step
  reduced the full gradient; the accumulated gradient is unchanged). Same W&B run id; the
  ~200 overlapping early steps are dropped by W&B. Planned next: a fused, same-precision
  target-head objective (teacher logits computed once per position instead of per anchor
  block, one log-softmax pass, analytic backward) swapped in at a checkpoint boundary via
  `training.resume_from`.
- 2026-10-15 16:56 UTC: at the first checkpoint (step 1196; the run was at step 1250, 5.95-6.09 s per
  step, compute 5.3 s, peak 39 GB) the job was stopped to resume with the fast head, but the config
  validator rejects `training.resume_from` under `deployment.managed_local` (resume is only wired for
  the external-mode consumer role; the managed producer cannot restart its prompt stream at the
  checkpoint's position). Restarted from scratch instead with the fast target-head objective
  (compiled) and a fresh W&B run id `dense-1-8-r4`; the superseded `dense-1-8-r3` run was deleted.
- 2026-10-15 17:04 UTC: running with the compiled fast head: 3.8-4.0 s per optimizer step
  (train compute 3.1-3.4 s, data wait 0.55-0.9 s) versus 5.95-6.1 s (compute 5.3 s) before; trainer
  peak memory 50 GB (39 GB before). The faster trainers now outran the two-quanta producer window
  (stop-and-go capture), so the run was restarted once more at 17:24 UTC with four quanta in flight
  (528 / 396; same W&B run id, the ~130 overlapping early steps are dropped by W&B).
- 2026-10-15 17:24 UTC (final launch): **2.95-3.0 s per optimizer step**, train compute 2.95 s, data
  wait 0.002 s, trainer peak memory 50.6 GB; `dspark_fast_head active: teacher table 3416 positions x
  151936 vocab (1.93 GiB)`. About 8.9 h per epoch, ~4.4 days for 12 epochs (ETA 2026-10-20).
